## Start Here: The Problem Looks Like 5 Different Problems

Read the problem statement again:

> Find the contiguous subarray with the largest sum.

A beginner's brain pattern-matches on surface words: "array," "maximum," "subarray" — and jumps to whatever pattern they learned most recently. That's how people wrongly reach for **Sliding Window**, **Prefix Sum + Brute Force**, or even **Two Pointers**.

The skill this repo is trying to build isn't "know Kadane's." It's: **train yourself to interrogate every keyword until only one pattern survives.**

---

## Keyword Autopsy — Change One Word, Change the Pattern

This is the core exercise. Take the original problem and mutate **one phrase at a time**. Watch how the correct pattern changes completely.

| Problem statement | Pattern | Why |
|---|---|---|
| "Max sum of any **contiguous subarray**" | **Kadane's** | Contiguous + no size limit + optimize a running sum → extend-or-restart decision |
| "Max sum of any contiguous subarray **of size k**" | **Sliding Window** | Fixed size k means you're not deciding *how much* to include — you're sliding a fixed window and doing O(1) add/remove at the edges |
| "Max sum of any **subsequence**" (order preserved, gaps allowed) | Different DP (depends on constraints) | Non-contiguous breaks Kadane's core assumption — you can't just "extend or restart," you now have a *choice per element*: include or exclude |
| "Max sum of any subarray, but the array is **circular**" | **Kadane's + trick (total − min subarray)** | Wrap-around breaks straightforward left-to-right scanning — you need a second pass |
| "Max **product** of any contiguous subarray" | **Kadane's variant (two running values)** | Multiplication flips sign behavior — a very negative running value can become the *best* value if multiplied by another negative. One variable is no longer enough. |
| "Find if there **exists** a subarray with sum = target" | **Prefix Sum + HashMap** | You're no longer optimizing a value — you're checking existence/equality. Totally different tool. |
| "Count the number of subarrays with sum = k" | **Prefix Sum + HashMap (counting)** | Again — counting, not optimizing. Kadane's has no notion of "count." |
| "Max sum of subarray with **at most k elements removed**" | DP with extra state dimension | Extra constraint = extra dimension in your DP state. Plain Kadane's has no room to track "removals used." |

**The meta-skill:** every one of these changes exactly **one constraint or one verb**, and that alone is enough to invalidate Kadane's. If you can't explain *why* a small wording change breaks the pattern, you don't understand the pattern — you've only memorized code.

