## The Decision Tree You Should Run In Your Head

Before writing a single line of code, ask these in order:

```
1. Is the required piece of the array CONTIGUOUS (subarray) or can elements
   be skipped (subsequence)?
   -> Subsequence  -> NOT Kadane's, likely a different DP (e.g. LIS, knapsack-style)
   -> Contiguous   -> continue to Q2

2. Is there a FIXED SIZE constraint (like "size k")?
   -> Yes -> Sliding Window
   -> No  -> continue to Q3

3. Am I OPTIMIZING a running value (max/min sum or product),
   or am I checking EXISTENCE/COUNT of some target sum?
   -> Existence/Count -> Prefix Sum + HashMap
   -> Optimizing      -> continue to Q4

4. Could the array contain negative numbers (or zero/negative products)?
   -> No  -> Trivial -- answer is the whole array (positives) -- you don't even need Kadane's
   -> Yes -> Kadane's Algorithm (or a Kadane's variant, see Q5)

5. Is it SUM or PRODUCT I'm optimizing?
   -> Sum     -> Standard Kadane's, ONE running variable
   -> Product -> Kadane's variant, TWO running variables (max AND min -- because
                 a very negative product can flip to the best value)
```

Run this tree on any new problem you meet. If you internalize nothing else from this repo, internalize this tree.

