## Why Brute Force Fails You Mentally (Not Just Speed-Wise)

The obvious brute force: check every possible subarray (`O(n²)` pairs of start/end, `O(n)` to sum each = `O(n³)`, or `O(n²)` with prefix sums).

The real reason to reject brute force isn't just "it's slow." It's that brute force treats every subarray as **independent** — you compute its sum from scratch, learning nothing from the subarray you just computed next to it.

**The insight that unlocks the optimization:** the best subarray ending at index `i` has a direct relationship to the best subarray ending at index `i-1`. You are *not* starting from zero each time — you're reusing work. That reuse-of-previous-computation is the fingerprint of **every DP problem**, not just this one. Train yourself to ask: *"Does solving position i give me a shortcut for position i+1?"* If yes, you're looking at DP, and brute force is the wrong lens entirely.

