## Near-Neighbor Patterns (The Ones That Trick People)

These patterns share surface vocabulary with Kadane's but require completely different logic. Interviewers deliberately phrase questions to sit right at these boundaries.

| Pattern | Shares with Kadane's | Key difference | Giveaway keyword |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Sliding Window** | Contiguous, scanning left to right | Fixed size / fixed constraint (e.g. "at most k distinct") | "size k," "at most k," "window" |
| **Prefix Sum + HashMap** | Sums over subarrays | Checks existence/count of a target, not optimization | "equals target," "count of," "exists" |
| **Two Pointers** | Scans an array with running state | Usually needs sorted input or a two-ended search | "sorted array," "pair," "two ends" |
| **DP with extra state (knapsack-style)** | Optimizes over an array | Has an extra constraint dimension (budget, count of operations) | "at most k removals," "with one skip allowed" |
| **Greedy** | Also O(n), single pass | No "extend or restart" — a locally optimal choice is provably globally optimal without needing to compare two options | "always choose the largest/smallest available" |

